<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Mainland Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Phitsanulok regime</h1><p class="st-meta">1767 CE – 1768 CE · capital Phitsanulok</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A breakaway regime in northern Siam led by the governor of Phitsanulok, one of the rivals Taksin had to overcome after Ayutthaya's fall.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the Burmese sack of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya Kingdom</a> in 1767, the governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a>, a Thai noble named Rueang, declared himself ruler of the northern provinces of the central plain, an area that included the old cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phichit">Phichit</a>. He took royal style as Chao Phitsanulok.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The regime controlled the upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phraya-river">Chao Phraya</a> and Nan river region, a rich <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>-growing area with strong defensive positions. It repelled an early attack by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> in 1768, during which Taksin was wounded. Little else is known of its administration, which probably continued the provincial structures of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Chao <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a> died in 1768, soon after crowning himself, and his territory was seized by the neighbouring <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sawangkhaburi-regime">Sawangkhaburi regime</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chao-phra-fang">Chao Phra Fang</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taksin">Taksin</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thonburi-kingdom">Thonburi Kingdom</a> conquered the whole north in 1770.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="other, 1767 CE – 1768 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path